# Noctra configuration.
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in your own values:
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored. Never commit real secrets. In Vercel, add the
# same names under Project → Settings → Environment Variables.

# ─── Firebase Authentication (browser) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Firebase Console → Project settings → Your apps → (your web app) → SDK setup
# and configuration. Every value below is a PUBLIC client identifier — it is
# bundled into the browser by design. Real security is enforced by verifying
# ID tokens server-side, not by hiding these.
#
# If any of these are missing or blank the app still builds and the landing
# page still renders, but Google sign-in is disabled and the dashboard shows a
# clear "Configuration required" message instead of crashing.

NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# ─── Firebase Admin SDK (server only) ────────────────────────────────────────
# NEVER prefix these with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# Used by `/api/history` to verify the Firebase ID token in the Authorization
# header. The UID is read from the *verified token*; a uid in a request body
# is ignored.
#
# FIREBASE_ADMIN_PROJECT_ID is the one value that is strictly required for
# verification: it must equal the project the tokens were issued by (for this
# app, noctra-a7423), or `verifyIdToken()` rejects every request with an
# audience mismatch. The client email/private key are only needed for admin
# operations beyond token verification — with a project ID alone the SDK falls
# back to Google's public certificates.
#
# Firebase Console → Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new private key.
# Generate these credentials for the same Firebase project that issues the
# browser tokens. For the current Noctra deployment that is `noctra-a7423`.
#
# FIREBASE_ADMIN_PROJECT_ID=
# FIREBASE_ADMIN_CLIENT_EMAIL=
# FIREBASE_ADMIN_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"

# ─── Supabase (server only) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NEVER prefix these with NEXT_PUBLIC_ — the server key bypasses row
# level security and would hand a browser full database access. A guard in
# `src/lib/supabase-config.ts` throws at startup if a NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed
# name is ever passed to it.
#
# Supabase Dashboard → Project Settings → API:
#   SUPABASE_URL              → Project URL
#   SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY       → modern `sb_secret_…` key (preferred)
#
# For older projects that still use legacy API keys, the fallback remains:
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY → legacy service_role secret (keep server-side)
#
# The browser never talks to Supabase: it calls `/api/history`, which verifies
# the Firebase ID token and then acts with this key. There is deliberately no
# public Supabase variable.
#
# If these are blank, the app still builds, converts and downloads normally —
# only saved history is unavailable, and the UI says so.
#
# SUPABASE_URL=
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
#
# Optional, and ONLY for `npm run verify:backend`: the public anon key. The app
# never uses it. Supplying it lets the verifier prove that row level security
# really does deny the public key, instead of merely assuming it.
#
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ─── Optional study assistant (server only) ──────────────────────────────────
# No provider is enabled by default. If configured, the browser must explicitly
# consent before selected document passages are sent to this endpoint. These
# values are never prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_. The endpoint may be any
# OpenAI-compatible /chat/completions service approved by the operator.
#
# NOCTRA_AI_BASE_URL=
# NOCTRA_AI_API_KEY=
# NOCTRA_AI_MODEL=
